WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.700
Chapter 03 quotes the mandates
rather than paraphrasing

2
00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:06.362
them into something
stronger, and so will I.

3
00:00:06.362 --> 00:00:12.767
OMB M-22-09 says agencies must resolve DNS
queries using encrypted DNS wherever it

4
00:00:12.767 --> 00:00:17.957
is technically supported, and should
explicitly configure endpoints to use

5
00:00:17.957 --> 00:00:21.281
agency-designated encrypted
DNS servers rather

6
00:00:21.281 --> 00:00:24.362
than relying on automatic
network discovery.

7
00:00:24.362 --> 00:00:29.289
CISA's Protective DNS is required
for federal civilian agencies,

8
00:00:29.289 --> 00:00:34.125
and CISA's guidance says it is used
for all egress DNS resolution.

9
00:00:34.125 --> 00:00:38.457
OMB M-26-14 rescinded M-21-31
and its event-logging tiers;

10
00:00:38.457 --> 00:00:40.383
it doesn't name DNS at all,

11
00:00:40.383 --> 00:00:46.545
but its baseline needs source and
destination network address information,

12
00:00:46.545 --> 00:00:52.225
and logs actively searchable for six
months and retrievable for a year.

13
00:00:52.225 --> 00:00:56.827
M-08-23 required DNSSEC by December 2009,

14
00:00:56.827 --> 00:01:04.637
and SC-20 and SC-21 carry signing
and validation into every baseline.

15
00:01:04.637 --> 00:01:10.250
M-21-07's IPv6-only
targets have all passed.

16
00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:17.213
And TIC 3.0, version 3.3, lists
five DNS enforcement capabilities.

17
00:01:17.213 --> 00:01:20.992
NIST SP 800-81 Revision
3, from March 2026,

18
00:01:20.992 --> 00:01:27.176
is the practical guide to meeting
them; it's guidance, not a mandate.

19
00:01:27.176 --> 00:01:32.266
Read together: agency-controlled
resolvers that log, encrypt, validate,

20
00:01:32.266 --> 00:01:36.925
forward egress through Protective
DNS, and can't be routed around.

21
00:01:36.925 --> 00:01:40.470
FedRAMP certifies a cloud
service, not an architecture,

22
00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:43.475
and the agency still
authorizes its own system.

23
00:01:43.475 --> 00:01:48.869
As an example, the Marketplace lists
Infoblox Government Cloud at Class C,

24
00:01:48.869 --> 00:01:53.025
its label for Moderate, delivered
as SaaS in AWS GovCloud.

25
00:01:53.025 --> 00:01:56.501
A Moderate certification
doesn't cover High or DoD,

26
00:01:56.501 --> 00:02:00.887
and on-premises appliances sit
inside the agency's own boundary.

27
00:02:00.887 --> 00:02:07.184
Every chapter follows one sequence:
inventory, coexist, migrate authority,

28
00:02:07.184 --> 00:02:13.175
federate clouds, decommission AD DNS
and DHCP, then harden and evidence.

29
00:02:13.175 --> 00:02:17.665
Five configuration decisions carry
most of the risk along the way;

30
00:02:17.665 --> 00:02:19.850
we'll meet each one in its module.
